requirements:
Implement in Rust as a SINGLE source file that compiles with `rustc --edition 2024`, using std only. Output ONLY the Rust source code inside ```rust fences. Do not use inner attributes or inner documentation comments.

All API types, fields, variants, and functions below must be public. Types must derive Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq; Section and Op, where present, must also derive Copy. The evaluator compiles the source as a test crate using `rustc --test --edition 2024`; no main function is needed.

Parse the three core components as u64. Do not trim input. Only ASCII digits are valid in a core component; do not accept signs, a v prefix, or whitespace. Empty entire input gives EmptyInput. Otherwise split the core on literal dots, preserving empty components: before inspecting components, require exactly three components or give ComponentCount { found: number_of_components }. Then inspect components in order major (index 0), minor (1), patch (2). Within each component check, in this order: empty => EmptyComponent; first non-ASCII-digit => InvalidDigit (offset is its zero-based UTF-8 BYTE offset within this component); more than one digit starting with zero => LeadingZero; value above u64::MAX => Overflow. Return the first error in this order. A single zero is valid; u64::MAX is valid. All component error indices are zero based. Core numeric validation precedes all identifier validation.

Exact public API (include the derives specified above):
```rust
pub struct Version { pub major: u64, pub minor: u64, pub patch: u64, pub prerelease: Vec<String>, pub build: Vec<String> }
pub enum Section { Prerelease, Build }
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ParseError {
    EmptyInput,
    ComponentCount { found: usize },
    EmptyComponent { index: usize },
    InvalidDigit { index: usize, offset: usize },
    LeadingZero { index: usize },
    Overflow { index: usize },
    EmptyIdentifier { section: Section, index: usize },
    InvalidIdentifier { section: Section, index: usize, offset: usize },
    NumericLeadingZero { index: usize },
}
pub fn parse_version(input: &str) -> Result<Version, ParseError>;
pub fn precedence(a: &Version, b: &Version) -> std::cmp::Ordering;
pub enum Op { Lt, Le, Eq, Ge, Gt }
pub struct Comparator { pub op: Op, pub version: Version, pub allows_prerelease: bool }
pub struct Range { pub alternatives: Vec<Vec<Comparator>> }
pub enum RangeError {
    EmptyAlternative { alternative: usize },
    InvalidVersion { alternative: usize, index: usize, error: ParseError },

}
pub fn parse_range(input: &str) -> Result<Range, RangeError>;
pub fn satisfies(version: &Version, range: &Range) -> bool;
```

Version syntax is MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH[-prerelease][+build]. Split on the FIRST '+' first, then on the FIRST '-' in the part before that '+'. Validate core, then prerelease, then build. A separator that is present introduces a section even if its text is empty. For each present section, split on '.' preserving empties, and inspect identifiers in order: empty => EmptyIdentifier { section, index }; first byte outside ASCII letters A-Z/a-z, digits 0-9, or '-' => InvalidIdentifier { section, index, offset }; then, ONLY for an all-digit prerelease identifier with length > 1 starting with '0', NumericLeadingZero { index }. Offsets are zero-based UTF-8 byte offsets WITHIN the identifier, and identifier indices are zero based within their section. A second '+' is thus an invalid build byte; additional '-' characters within either section are allowed. Numeric build identifiers may have leading zeros. Numeric prerelease identifiers have arbitrary length and do not overflow u64. Preserve spelling, case, ordering, and duplicates in the vectors; an absent section is an empty vector. Build content such as a hyphen is ordinary build text.

precedence returns std::cmp::Ordering. Compare (major, minor, patch) numerically first. At equal core, a release (no prerelease) is greater than any prerelease. Compare prerelease identifiers left to right: two numeric identifiers compare by mathematical numeric value (arbitrary length), numeric is less than nonnumeric, and two nonnumeric identifiers compare by case-sensitive ASCII lexicographic order. If all compared identifiers tie, the shorter prerelease list is less. Build metadata NEVER affects precedence. Version's derived Eq remains structural and therefore includes build metadata. Ordering and range functions need only handle Versions returned by parse_version or valid values with equivalent invariants.

Ranges are an OR of alternatives separated by the literal two-character string '||' (spaces around it are optional); within each alternative, tokens are separated by ASCII whitespace (space, tab, LF, CR, vertical tab, form feed), and all expanded comparators are ANDed. Unicode whitespace is not a separator. Entire empty/ASCII-whitespace-only input is one empty comparator set. For other inputs, process alternatives from left to right and give EmptyAlternative for an empty alternative when reached; process its tokens left to right and return the first encountered error. alternative and index are zero-based alternative number and whitespace-token number within it; errors do not use byte offsets at the range level. A single '|' is ordinary token content, not an OR separator.

Operator tokens begin with >=, <=, >, <, or = (choose the longest matching prefix); the remainder must be a full version accepted by parse_version. There can be NO whitespace between an operator and version. A missing or malformed remainder gives InvalidVersion with the unchanged nested ParseError. >, >=, <, <= compare precedence; = means equal precedence, including equality regardless of build metadata. Contradictory sets are valid and match nothing. Preserve alternative and comparator order; do not sort, deduplicate, or simplify.

For each alternative, first all its comparator relations must hold. In addition, a candidate with a nonempty prerelease is eligible ONLY if that SAME alternative has a comparator whose allows_prerelease is true and whose version has the same (major, minor, patch) tuple as the candidate. Comparator operators do not affect eligibility. A stable candidate needs no such opt-in. The OR result is true if any alternative both passes its relations and admits the candidate. An empty set matches all stable versions and excludes all prereleases. Eligibility never transfers across alternatives. Build metadata does not affect matching. satisfies must also follow these rules for manually constructed Ranges with valid Versions, using the explicitly supplied allows_prerelease flags.

At this level, a token without an operator is also a full version and expands to one Eq comparator. Malformed bare tokens give InvalidVersion with parse_version's error. Every comparator created by parse_range has allows_prerelease=true exactly when its parsed full version has prerelease identifiers. Shorthand, wildcard, hyphen, and partial versions are not supported.
